  "mechanismSummary": "Acid protonates the OH group and water leaves, giving a secondary carbocation. A methyl group on the next carbon moves across with its bonding electrons (a 1,2-methyl shift), giving a more stable tertiary cation. Losing a proton then gives mostly 2,3-dimethylbut-2-ene, the tetrasubstituted alkene, with some 2,3-dimethylbut-1-ene. The unrearranged 3,3-dimethylbut-1-ene is only a few per cent. The shift and the loss of water may also happen together.",
